ic Shift Right (ASR) instruction twice sequentially on this register. What is the final decimal value stored in the register? Provide the answer as a base-10 integer, including the sign if negative.","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:4","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"4","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","machine-instructions","shift-and-rotate-operations","twos-complement","numerical-answers","goclasses-coa-topic-test"],"marks":1,"type":"NAT","answer":-12,"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>An 8-bit register contains the 2's complement representation of the decimal number $-48$. The processor executes an Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR) instruction twice sequentially on this register. What is the final decimal value stored in the register? Provide the answer as a base-10 integer, including the sign if negative.</p>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"c892d6c5-a322-4115-920b-e55436d3e4cb","journalQuestionId":"d0184421-9f39-4687-9005-c9ce63a05109","reattemptId":"b387fc2b-4e82-4081-93f9-7a838e106d03","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T05:33:58.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T05:37:16.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":198000,"number":"5","selectedAnswer":192,"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":198,"outcome":"RBS","questionText":"Consider an 8-bit register containing the binary value $X = 10010110_2$. Let $Y$ be the value resulting from a Logical Shift Right (LSR) by 2 bits on $X$. Let $Z$ be the value resulting from an Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR) by 2 bits on the original value $X$. Calculate the absolute difference between the unsigned decimal values of $Z$ and $Y$, i.e. $|Z-Y|$.","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:5","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"5","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","machine-instructions","shift-and-rotate-operations","numerical-answers","goclasses-coa-topic-test"],"marks":1,"type":"NAT","answer":192,"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Consider an 8-bit register containing the binary value $X = 10010110_2$. Let $Y$ be the value resulting from a Logical Shift Right (LSR) by 2 bits on $X$. Let $Z$ be the value resulting from an Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR) by 2 bits on the original value $X$. Calculate the absolute difference between the unsigned decimal values of $Z$ and $Y$, i.e. $|Z-Y|$.</p>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"8124b379-8984-463a-885a-c2cf3bcb9641","journalQuestionId":"646e0c29-7577-455e-9f72-de0646bfad1e","reattemptId":"46600a66-019b-4900-b5bd-f96131bbe5a2","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T05:37:21.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T05:41:52.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":271000,"number":"6","selectedAnswer":4992,"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":false,"timeSpentSec":271,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"Assume an instruction set that uses a fixed 16-bit instruction length. Operand specifiers are 6 bits in length. If the instruction set supports exactly 12 two-operand instructions and 200 zero-operand instructions, what is the maximum number of one-operand instructions that can be supported?","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:6","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"6","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","machine-instructions","instruction-format","expanding-opcode","numerical-answers","goclasses-coa-topic-test"],"marks":2,"type":"NAT","answer":252,"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Assume an instruction set that uses a fixed 16-bit instruction length. Operand specifiers are 6 bits in length. If the instruction set supports exactly 12 two-operand instructions and 200 zero-operand instructions, what is the maximum number of one-operand instructions that can be supported?</p>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"ee5591bd-68e4-4d49-9a9d-8ba19f4ef627","journalQuestionId":"f7b855d6-fe28-4174-9a03-4fd425aec718","reattemptId":"d95c2e50-8db7-4e62-a95d-863e376b4b3c","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T05:41:57.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T05:48:01.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":364000,"number":"7","selectedAnswer":["A","C"],"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":false,"timeSpentSec":364,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"Consider a machine architecture where we want to determine if it is possible to design an expanding opcode. Assume:\nInstruction length: 12 bits\nRegister operand size: 3 bits\nWe wish to encode:\n4 instructions with 3 registers\n255 instructions with 1 register\n16 instructions with 0 registers\nBased on the bit-pattern capacity method for evaluating expanding opcodes, which statements are correct?\nA. The first 4 instructions (with 3 registers) account for exactly $4 \\times 2^9 = 2048$ bit patterns in the overall instruction space.\nB. The 255 instructions with 1 register account for exactly 255 bit patterns since they only utilize a single operand.\nC. The total maximum capacity of a 12-bit instruction format is $2^{12} = 4096$ unique bit patterns.\nD. The proposed instruction set cannot be encoded because the total required bit patterns (4104) exceed the available capacity.","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:7","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"7","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","machine-instructions","instruction-format","expanding-opcode","multiple-selects","goclasses-coa-topic-test"],"marks":2,"type":"MSQ","answer":["A","C","D"],"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Consider a machine architecture where we want to determine if it is possible to design an expanding opcode. Assume:</p><ul><li>Instruction length: 12 bits</li><li>Register operand size: 3 bits</li></ul><p>We wish to encode:</p><ul><li>4 instructions with 3 registers</li><li>255 instructions with 1 register</li><li>16 instructions with 0 registers</li></ul><p>Based on the bit-pattern capacity method for evaluating expanding opcodes, which statements are correct?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>The first 4 instructions (with 3 registers) account for exactly $4 \\times 2^9 = 2048$ bit patterns in the overall instruction space.</li><li>The 255 instructions with 1 register account for exactly 255 bit patterns since they only utilize a single operand.</li><li>The total maximum capacity of a 12-bit instruction format is $2^{12} = 4096$ unique bit patterns.</li><li>The proposed instruction set cannot be encoded because the total required bit patterns (4104) exceed the available capacity.</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"0f10ed6e-95df-4e5e-8049-597a76a674ad","journalQuestionId":"9e34eca2-6932-41e1-b43a-82f18c46ebd8","reattemptId":"04aa01d8-ec04-423e-b5da-7a68d47c5767","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T05:48:06.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T05:53:56.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":350000,"number":"8","selectedAnswer":["A","C","D"],"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":false,"timeSpentSec":350,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"Consider a processor utilizing a 24-bit instruction format and a register file with 64 general-purpose registers. The processor supports 3-, 2-, 1-, and 0-operand instructions exclusively using an expanding opcode technique. Let $N_k$ represent the number of $k$-operand instructions encoded in the system.\nWhich statements regarding the theoretical limits of this instruction set are always true?\nA. If the processor implements exactly $N_3 = 63$, the absolute maximum possible value for $N_2$ is 64.\nB. The expansion factor (the multiplier for available opcodes) at every transition from $k$ to $k-1$ operands is exactly 64.\nC. If the processor implements $N_3 = 64$, it becomes impossible to implement any instructions with fewer operands.\nD. If the processor implements $N_3 = 62$ and $N_2 = 64$, the available capacity for 1-operand instructions is exactly 4096.","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:8","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"8","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","machine-instructions","instruction-format","expanding-opcode","multiple-selects","goclasses-coa-topic-test"],"marks":2,"type":"MSQ","answer":["A","B","C","D"],"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Consider a processor utilizing a 24-bit instruction format and a register file with 64 general-purpose registers. The processor supports 3-, 2-, 1-, and 0-operand instructions exclusively using an expanding opcode technique. Let $N_k$ represent the number of $k$-operand instructions encoded in the system.</p><p>Which statements regarding the theoretical limits of this instruction set are always true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>If the processor implements exactly $N_3 = 63$, the absolute maximum possible value for $N_2$ is 64.</li><li>The expansion factor (the multiplier for available opcodes) at every transition from $k$ to $k-1$ operands is exactly 64.</li><li>If the processor implements $N_3 = 64$, it becomes impossible to implement any instructions with fewer operands.</li><li>If the processor implements $N_3 = 62$ and $N_2 = 64$, the available capacity for 1-operand instructions is exactly 4096.</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"7cb78dac-67be-4fe0-890c-3b3ec30bf12c","journalQuestionId":"fadc9af0-1d1e-4cc3-881d-84ab82bce3bc","reattemptId":"97a65443-3789-48e0-b547-d57214592195","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T05:54:01.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T05:56:16.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":135000,"number":"9","selectedAnswer":["A","B"],"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":false,"timeSpentSec":135,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"Consider an 8-bit microprocessor with a 16-bit address bus. An address decoder selects a specific memory block when the top three address bits $(A_{15}, A_{14}, A_{13})$ are set to $101_2$. The remaining 13 bits ($x$) address individual locations within this memory block, forming the address pattern $101xxxxxxxxxxxxx_2$.\nWhich statements are true regarding this memory configuration?\nA. The lowest accessible address in this memory block is $A000_{16}$.\nB. The highest accessible address in this memory block is $BFFF_{16}$.\nC. The total size of this specific memory block is 8 KB.\nD. Decoding the top three address lines divides the entire 16-bit address space into exactly 4 independent blocks.","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:9","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"9","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"memory-chip-design"},"topic":"Memory Chip Design","topicSlug":"memory-chip-design","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","memory-chip-design","memory-interfacing","address-decoding","multiple-selects","goclasses-coa-topic-test"],"marks":2,"type":"MSQ","answer":["A","B","C"],"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Consider an 8-bit microprocessor with a 16-bit address bus. An address decoder selects a specific memory block when the top three address bits $(A_{15}, A_{14}, A_{13})$ are set to $101_2$. The remaining 13 bits ($x$) address individual locations within this memory block, forming the address pattern $101xxxxxxxxxxxxx_2$.</p><p>Which statements are true regarding this memory configuration?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>The lowest accessible address in this memory block is $A000_{16}$.</li><li>The highest accessible address in this memory block is $BFFF_{16}$.</li><li>The total size of this specific memory block is 8 KB.</li><li>Decoding the top three address lines divides the entire 16-bit address space into exactly 4 independent blocks.</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"ac3a1fac-b588-4ca9-abd6-a24c43879a28","journalQuestionId":"f545fa3d-8c7d-420d-a0f7-55189e4e57d6","reattemptId":"ddb7b7e1-4511-4ce8-b346-aed7a65af12f","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T05:56:21.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T05:59:45.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":204000,"number":"10","selectedAnswer":["A"],"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":false,"timeSpentSec":204,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"A 16-bit microprocessor addresses a 32 KB block of ROM mapped contiguously starting from the base memory address $0000_{16}$. The 32 KB ROM block is constructed using four separate 8 KB memory chips (Chip 0 through Chip 3). Which statements regarding the memory interfacing and addressing of this system are true?\nA. Each 8 KB memory chip requires exactly 13 address lines ($A_0$ to $A_{12}$) from the processor to select a specific byte within the chip.\nB. The valid memory address range serviced by the third chip (Chip 2) is exactly $4000_{16}$ to $5FFF_{16}$.\nC. To properly decode and select exactly one of the four 8 KB chips during a memory access within the 32 KB block, address lines $A_{13}$ and $A_{14}$ can be connected to the select inputs of a 2-to-4 decoder.\nD. During any valid memory access to this 32 KB ROM block, the highest-order address bit ($A_{15}$) must be at a logic '1' state.","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:10","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"10","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"memory-chip-design"},"topic":"Memory Chip Design","topicSlug":"memory-chip-design","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","memory-chip-design","memory-interfacing","address-decoding","multiple-selects","goclasses-coa-topic-test"],"marks":2,"type":"MSQ","answer":["A","B","C"],"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>A 16-bit microprocessor addresses a 32 KB block of ROM mapped contiguously starting from the base memory address $0000_{16}$. The 32 KB ROM block is constructed using four separate 8 KB memory chips (Chip 0 through Chip 3). Which statements regarding the memory interfacing and addressing of this system are true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Each 8 KB memory chip requires exactly 13 address lines ($A_0$ to $A_{12}$) from the processor to select a specific byte within the chip.</li><li>The valid memory address range serviced by the third chip (Chip 2) is exactly $4000_{16}$ to $5FFF_{16}$.</li><li>To properly decode and select exactly one of the four 8 KB chips during a memory access within the 32 KB block, address lines $A_{13}$ and $A_{14}$ can be connected to the select inputs of a 2-to-4 decoder.</li><li>During any valid memory access to this 32 KB ROM block, the highest-order address bit ($A_{15}$) must be at a logic '1' state.</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"3f45d449-774a-42c0-93ea-4d0d87381cd6","journalQuestionId":"229c09e1-b910-4b5a-81c6-d7e51c4f1ccb","reattemptId":"4ab7aed3-c645-4b0a-ab62-a428433e4f4c","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T05:59:50.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T05:59:53.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":3000,"number":"11","selectedAnswer":null,"decision":"SKIP","markCorrect":null,"timeSpentSec":3,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"An 8-bit microprocessor with a 16-bit address bus interfaces with a 32 KB block of ROM mapped contiguously starting from memory address $0000_{16}$. The ROM block is composed of four 8 KB chips (Chip 0 through Chip 3 in contiguous order). A 2-to-4 line decoder generates the active-low Chip Select ($\\overline{CS}$) signals for the four chips. Address line $A_{14}$ is connected to the MSB select input and $A_{13}$ to the LSB select input of the decoder. If the microprocessor executes a read operation to fetch data from hexadecimal address $6F3A_{16}$, what is the decimal value of the decoder output line (0 to 3) that becomes active?","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:11","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"11","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"memory-chip-design"},"topic":"Memory Chip Design","topicSlug":"memory-chip-design","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","memory-chip-design","memory-interfacing