 hexadecimal address ranges for DRAM 1 and DRAM 2, respectively?\nA. DRAM 1: $800000$ to $9FFFFF$; DRAM 2: $A00000$ to $BFFFFF$\nB. DRAM 1: $800000$ to $BFFFFF$; DRAM 2: $C00000$ to $FFFFFF$\nC. DRAM 1: $800000$ to $8FFFFF$; DRAM 2: $900000$ to $9FFFFF$\nD. DRAM 1: $800000$ to $FFFFFF$; DRAM 2: $000000$ to $7FFFFF$","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test:12","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"12","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"memory-chip-design"},"topic":"Memory Chip Design","topicSlug":"memory-chip-design","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","memory-chip-design","memory-interfacing","address-decoding","goclasses-coa-topic-test"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"B","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Consider a 68K microprocessor system with a 24-bit address bus ($A_{23}$ to $A_0$) and a 16-bit data bus. The system implements:</p><ul><li>A 1 Mbyte ROM block starting at address $000000_{16}$.</li><li>An 8 Mbyte DRAM block starting at address $800000_{16}$.</li></ul><pre>Memory organization\nROM:  2 sub-blocks × (256K × 16-bit) = 1 Mbyte\nDRAM: 2 banks × 4 parallel (2M × 4-bit) chips = 8 Mbytes</pre><p>The 8 Mbyte DRAM space mapped from address $800000_{16}$ is divided into two equal 4 Mbyte sub-blocks, DRAM 1 and DRAM 2. DRAM 1 is selected when $A_{23}=1$ and $A_{22}=0$; DRAM 2 is selected when $A_{23}=1$ and $A_{22}=1$. What are the correct hexadecimal address ranges for DRAM 1 and DRAM 2, respectively?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>DRAM 1: $800000$ to $9FFFFF$; DRAM 2: $A00000$ to $BFFFFF$</li><li>DRAM 1: $800000$ to $BFFFFF$; DRAM 2: $C00000$ to $FFFFFF$</li><li>DRAM 1: $800000$ to $8FFFFF$; DRAM 2: $900000$ to $9FFFFF$</li><li>DRAM 1: $800000$ to $FFFFFF$; DRAM 2: $000000$ to $7FFFFF$</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes 